From 36042d4a2151e6820b8870b8d98db60ae0436f8a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 00:27:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][dvbridge] The IPT tunnel converts each pixel once, in
 one compute pass

Pass 9 (the IPT tunnel, an expert choice) took the conversion of the
left pixel of each pair twice: in 9a for its I and in 9b for the P and
T of the pair. In 9a, the even and the odd pixels of a group of
invocations also ran both sides of a branch (the 12-tap chroma filter
of the odd pixel).

Add 9c, a compute form, used where OpenGL ES 3.1 makes it and the
compute forms are asked for (dvcomputerows, on by default). Each
invocation makes one texel of the rows: two pairs on two rows. Each
pixel is converted once (the even one gives its I and the P and T of
its pair), and the chroma of the odd pixels takes one fetch of each of
the 12 texels of the filter for both rows. 9a and 9b stay as they were,
for a GPU that cannot make 9c or where it fails (then never again), and
kodi.log says which is used.

Measured with the software bridge on the 14 pictures of a profile 7
FEL stream, against the chain in double precision:
- 9a and 9b: the same bytes as before.
- 9c: P, T and the I of the odd pixels are the same bytes. The I of
  0.003 % of the even pixels moves by one code: the GPU fuses a
  multiply and an add of the OETF where only I uses it, and not where
  P and T share it. Overall I is 0.042 % one code off (0.043 % before),
  P 0.029 % and T 0.036 % as before, never more than one code.
- Pass 9 of a 4K frame on llvmpipe: about 63 ms instead of 116.
